Do Broglie relation \lambda=$\frac{h}{\mathrm{P}} ...(1) Energy conservation, \mathrm{qV}=\mathrm{K} ...(2) Energy vs momentum relation K=\frac{p^2}{2 m} ...(3) Using equation (1), (2)$ and $(3) \begin{aligned}& \lambda=\frac{\mathrm{h}}{\mathrm{p}}=\frac{\mathrm{h}}{\sqrt{2 \mathrm{Km}}}\\
& \lambda=\frac{\mathrm{h}}{\sqrt{2 \mathrm{qVm}}}
\end{aligned}$ Now, for proton of mass $M$ that is accelerated through $9 \mathrm{ V}$, the de-broglie wavelength can be written as, $\begin{aligned}& \lambda_{\mathrm{P}}=\frac{\mathrm{h}}{\sqrt{2 \mathrm{q}(9 \mathrm{ V}) \mathrm{M}}}\\
& \therefore \frac{\lambda}{\lambda_{\mathrm{p}}}=3 \sqrt{\frac{\mathrm{M}}{\mathrm{m}}}\\
